# Who pays a ticket tax?
subtitle: "Why a $10 ticket tax is split by who can walk away more easily, not by who hands the money to the government."
thumb: card cork

## Who pays a ticket tax?
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Tickets sold
    y_label: Price ($)
    demand:
      curvature: linear
      elasticity: 1
    supply:
      curvature: linear
      elasticity: 1
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
    tax:
      amount: 10
      side: seller
      kind: per_unit
    regions:
      - government_revenue
    units:
      price: [0, 100]
      quantity: [0, 1000]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: tickets
    readout: []
details:
    tax_supply_curve: 'A per-unit tax of $t collected from sellers shifts supply up by exactly $t: to supply a ticket, a seller now needs the old price plus the tax.'
    supply_curve: 'With a fixed number of seats, the quantity offered hardly responds to price in the short run: supply is nearly perfectly inelastic, so almost all of a tax falls on sellers.'

    text t_fans "Fans pay ${market.price_buyers}" #b5482e x 760 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    text t_sell "Sellers keep ${market.price_sellers}" below t_fans gap 22 #2f6b8f font_size 30 hidden true
    text t_before "even split: $55 / $45" below t_sell gap 18 #8f887c font_size 24 hidden true
    mark mark_ps around t_sell2 kind box hidden true

    card title "Who pays a ticket tax?"

When the government taxes every concert ticket by ten dollars, who really pays it?
    draw axes

Start with the ticket market before any tax: fans on one side…
    draw demand_curve label_D

…sellers on the other.
    draw supply_curve label_S

They meet at fifty dollars a ticket.
    draw equilibrium_point guide_lines label_E label_Pstar

    pause 0.4

Now the law says the seller must send ten dollars per ticket to the government, so the whole supply curve lifts by ten.
    draw tax_supply_curve label_S_tax

The seller would love to just add ten dollars to the price.
    point at.data [50,60] gesture tap

But at sixty dollars some fans stay home, so the price settles in between.
    draw tax_equilibrium_point tax_guide_lines

Fans now pay fifty-five, and sellers keep forty-five.
    draw price_buyers_point label_Pb price_sellers_point label_Ps t_fans t_sell

The ten-dollar gap between those two prices is the government's cut, split five and five.
    draw wedge_region label_wedge

    pause 0.4

That even split came from assuming both sides can back out equally easily.
    draw t_before

A concert is different: the hall has the same number of seats tonight, whatever the price.
    highlight supply_curve

So the supply curve is about to stand up straight.
    animate supply.elasticity 0.06 duration 3.5 ghost ["supply_curve","tax_supply_curve","price_buyers_point","price_sellers_point"]

    erase label_S_tax t_fans t_sell t_before label_Pb label_Ps

    text t_fans2 "Fans pay $50.50" #b5482e x 760 y 470 font_size 30
    text t_sell2 "Sellers keep $40.50" below t_fans2 gap 22 #2f6b8f font_size 30
    parallel true

Now the fans' price barely moves above fifty.
    highlight price_buyers_point t_fans2

And the sellers' take drops to just over forty: they carry almost the whole tax.
    highlight price_sellers_point t_sell2 delivery grave

Empty seats earn nothing, so the promoter cannot refuse the lower price the way a fan can refuse a higher one.
    focus supply_curve tax_supply_curve

Whoever has fewer ways out pays more of the tax, no matter who writes the cheque to the government.
    draw mark_ps

    quiz "With a fixed number of seats, who bears most of the $10 ticket tax?"
        right: The sellers: with the seats fixed they cannot walk away, so the price fans pay barely rises and the tax comes out of what sellers keep.
        * The fans, because they buy the tickets
        + The sellers, who cannot cut the seats
        * It is always split fifty-fifty
        * The government, since it collects it
---
